1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gustaf Eneskjöld, † 1719-01-00 in Russian captivity in Moscow. Freerider at the Turku and Pori County Cavalry Regiment 1674. Corporal at the Turku and Pori County Cavalry Regiment 1675-02-00. Adjutant 1675-07-12. C
2 CONC ornet 23-12-1675. Lieutenant 1676-11-00. Captain lieutenant 1678-03-27. Horsemaster 27-11-1678. Confirmation power of attorney 14-10-1679. Major 11-11-1700. Colonel and head of the regiment 1701-08-07. He witnessed the battle of N
2 CONC arva, was captured at Errastfer 1701-12-30, exchanged in 1708, again captured at Poltava 1709-06-28. Married 1680-07-11 in Nikolai parish, Stockholm (Wn.) to baroness Hilgard Elisabet Rehbinder born 11-10-1663, † 11-12-1735. Daugh
2 CONC ter of the colonel and baron Bernhard Rehbinder and his 1st wife Elisabet Munck af Fulkila. Children: Helena Elisabeth. Married 1:o 1713 to commander Samuel Chytreen, † 1720-09-11 at Herrala in Lempäälä. Married 2nd 1721-06-15 at 
2 CONC Herrala to the rust holder Matias Tammelin born 1690, † 1764-11-12 in his 1st marriage. Bernt Johan Eneskjöld born at Lyttskär and baptized 11/11/1683 baptized there, † 1726. See Tab. 7 ex. TAB 3 Carl Magnus Eneskjöld born 14-11-1
2 CONC 688? and baptized 1689-01-14 at Lyttskär. Entered service in 1700. Corporal at the Turku and Bjornborg county cavalry regiment. Second Cornet at the Turku and Pori County Cavalry Regiment 1707-02-22. Second lieutenant 1709-04-05. 
2 CONC Captured 1709-09-01 at Perevolotjna. † unmarried 1721-07-00 in Totma during Russian captivity. Gustaf Fredrik Eneskjöld born 1685 at Lyttskär, † 1686-02-28. Elisabet Dorotea Eneskjöld born at Lyttskär and baptized there 1686-04-29
2 CONC , probably † 1694-05-05 at Lyttskär. Gustaf Eneskjöld born 1687 at Lyttskär, † 1764. See Tab. 8 ex. TAB 4 Charlotta Eneskjöld born at Lyttskär and baptized there 15-11-1692, † 08-05-1740 at Lyttskär. She received support from the 
2 CONC refugee commission in 1716. Married to the master of horse at the Turku county cavalry regiment Johan Ludvig Taube, † around 1716 in Russian captivity. Anna Christina Eneskjöld born on Lyttskär and baptized there 1691-07-28, † 176
2 CONC 0-06-14, Married to schoutbynachten Evert Johan Taube baptized 1681, † 1759. Otto Reinhold Eneskjöld born 1694-06-29 on Lyttskär, died unmarried in the battle at Storkyro 1714-02-19. Field sergeant at the Bjornborg county infantry
2 CONC  regiment. Ensign at Björneborg county's infantry regiment 1712-03-02. Henrik Johan Eneskjöld born 1697, † 1743. See Tab. 9 Anna Hildgard Eneskjöld born 1699-09-19 in Lyttskär, † 1768-03-21 in Vallby, Hagby parish, Uppsala county 
2 CONC and buried 1768-04-02 in Husby-Lyhundra church, Stockholm county. Married to her brother-in-law's cousin, Major Jakob Rudolf Taube, in his 2nd marriage, born 1688, † 1766.
2 CONT Lähde: https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Eneskj%C3%B6ld_nr_585
2 CONT Captured in Errastfer 1701-12-30
2 CONT
2 CONT Released in the exchange of prisoners in 1708, imprisoned again in Pultava on 1709-06-28
2 CONT
2 CONT The owner of the Lyttylä manor: "Already in 1655, Engelbrekt Eneskiöld had received a mortgage in the engagement court on a 3 1/2 eyry-sized part of Lyttylä, which he had bought from the heirs of the former owner, Maalina Antintyt
2 CONC äre in Ruohoniemi and Matti Antinpoja in Hyve-
2 CONT here. After Engelbrekt Eneskiöld l) died, Lyttylä had to be his
2 CONT to his son Kustaa Eneskiöld's. On August 12, 1682,
2 CONT with the help of the king with a letter, he had his ownership rights confirmed.
2 CONT Soon after this, however, the reduction was extended to the little ones
2 CONT tilus and Lyttylä were ordered to cancel the year 1682
2 CONT with tax. However, the space was immediately given back to ''Kustaa Eneskiöldin''
2 CONT to cultivate for life. Kustaa Eneskiöld died in 1715 and
2 CONT after him, Lyttylä was ruled successively by Eneskiöld-
2 CONT related persons: Captain Berndt J u h a n a Eneskiöld 2) († 1726),
2 CONT major Berndt Juhana Eneskiöld 3) († 1777) and lieutenant Berndt
2 CONT Kustaa Eneskiöld 4) († 1829)." Source: Satakunta, kotisetutuikkija I, published by Satakuntalainen osakunta, in Helsinki 1907, OSAKEYHTIÖ KAUPPAKRIJAPAINO. 173. https://julkaisut.satakuntalainenosakunta.fi/satakuntasarja/pdf/Satak
2 CONC untasarja_I.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fi.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yyterin_kartano
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.poritieto.com/index.php/Yyterin_kyl%C3%A4_ja_Yyterin_kartano
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fi.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suuri_Pohjan_sota
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.helsinki.fi/ylioppilasmatrikkeli/henkilo.php?id=1824
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Eneskj%C3%B6ld_nr_585#TAB_2
2 CONT
2 CONT http://runeberg.org/anrep/1/0746.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Kustaa(Gustaf) Eneskiöld, d.1718 in captivity. Served as master of cavalry in the Turku county cavalry regiment in 1678 and as major in 1700., was promoted to colonel in 1701. He was imprisoned in 1701, but returned in 1707.-pso H
2 CONC ildegard Rehbinder Participated as the commander of the Turku cavalry regiment in the great bottom war in the forces of Charles XII. .During the siege of the city of Pultava, 15,000 soldiers were captured by the Russians, includin
2 CONC g Kustaa and his son captain Berndt Johan and lieutenants Gustaf and Carl Magnus (died in captivity 1719). 1722 Berndt (see X) Johan and Gustaf (see Y) returned from captivity after 13 years of forced labor. Gustaf continued in th
2 CONC e service and Yleni became a captain, resigned from the service in 1745 and died in 1764. The youngest brother Otto fought as a lieutenant in the Pori infantry regiment. He died in 1714 in the forces of Major General Arnfelt in Os
2 CONC trobothnia in the Battle of Napue.  
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Gustav k. January. 1719 in Moscow as a prisoner of war. Colonel (Family No. 585).
2 CONT
2 CONT University of Helsinki: Student register 1640–1852: "Gustaf Eneskjöld Vht: landowner in Pori Lyttylä, chamberlain Engelbrekt Nilsson Eneskjöld († 1664) and Elisabet Grothusen. Student in Turku 1665/66 for the register of the Satak
2 CONC unta section [1665/66] Free horseman in the Turku and Pori county cavalry regiment 1674, corporal 1675, adjutant s.v., cornet s.v., lieutenant 1676, captain-lieutenant 1678, cavalry master s.v., major 1700, colonel and regimental 
2 CONC commander 1701, became a prisoner of war in Errastfer s.v., released in 1708. Prisoner again in Pultava 1709. Owned by Porin Lyttylän, Y Yteri and Tuorsniemi and Ahlainen Kellahden. † as a prisoner of war in Moscow1.1719.
2 CONT
2 CONT PORITIETO: "In 1705, colonel Rehbinder's son-in-law, colonel Gustav Eneskiöld, became the owner of Yyter's cavalry farm, who owned the farm until his death in 1715. Colonel Gustav Eneskiöld also owned the tax farm of Kellahde and 
2 CONC his family grave was under the old church of Pori. His widow, free lord Hildegard Rehbinder, was the owner of the farm until 1725. Freewoman Rehbinder was a prominent person in the highest social circles of Pori. She had the first
2 CONC  place of honor in the old church of Pori, where there was a silver crucifix that she donated."
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.paltta.fi/suku/paltta/paltta-aa7.htm "10 children were born in Lyttskär and Helena, with a question mark in front. At least two died young, 3 daughters married a man from the Taube family, all 5 sons were in the militar
2 CONC y - among them, Captain Bernt (1683 - 1726) was a prisoner in Tobolsk after the battle of Poltava, as well as Gustaf Fredrik, born 1687, Carl Magnus, born 1688, lieutenant, was captured in 1709 in Perevolotjna and died unmarried a
2 CONC s a prisoner of the Russians in Totjma in 1721, Otto Reinhold, born in 1694 , a lieutenant, fell unmarried in Isokyrö 1714 and Henrik Johan (1697 - 1743), was a lieutenant."
2 CONT
2 CONT Timo Kause: Rustholl's golden ducat, 2004: "Gustav served as a horse master in the Turku county cavalry regiment on 07.11.1678 and as a major of the same unit on 11.11.1700. He participated in several battles in Narva and was capt
2 CONC ured by the Russians in the battle of Errestfer (Erastfer) in Livonia on 01.01.1701. He participated in 1703 - 07 eagerly for the exchange of prisoners and in autumn 1707 he was sent to Novgorod, from where he was able to travel h
2 CONC ome on December 2, 1707 and was allowed to stay when the Russian colonel Alexander Gordon was released in February 1708. He was promoted to colonel on 07.08.1701 married in Stockholm on 11.07.1680 to freewoman Hildgard Elisabeth R
2 CONC ehbinder, who was the daughter of freewoman Bernhard Rehbinder and Elisabeth Munck af Fulkila. Although Gustaf Eneskiöld owned Lyttylä, Kellahde and Yyter, he lived in the town of Pori right next to the town hall in 1696. He and h
2 CONC is spouse free lord Hildgard Elisabeth Rehbinder were generous towards the church.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1709, Colonel Gustaf Eneskiöld participated in the Battle of Pultava as the commander of the Turku and Pori county cavalry regiment in the forces of Charles XII. In this battle, Peter the Great of Russia, with his superiority, 
2 CONC defeated Charles XII of Sweden, who fled to Turkey, leaving the main part of the army at the mercy of the enemy. Gustaf Eneskiöld was captured by the Russians for the second time. Four of his sons were captured in the same battle:
2 CONC  Captain Berndt Johan Eneskiöld, Lieutenants Gustaf Eneskiöld, Carl Magnus Eneskiöld and Otto Reinhold Eneskiöld, and their cousin Master of the Horse Engelbregt Johan von Jordan.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a result of the war, bigotry started. The Russians occupied, robbed and raped all of Finland. All men in the family who were able to fight were prisoners of the Russians. Gustaf Eneskiöld and his sons were all dispersed to diff
2 CONC erent forced labor camps across vast Russia. Of the boys, Gustaf was sent to Solikamsk, Carl Magnus to Totma and Berndt Johan to Tobolsk in Siberia. There is no information about Otto Reinhold's fate. He disappeared into the expan
2 CONC ses of vast Russia. Only Berndt Johan managed to get to the same place as his cousin Engelbregt von Jordan, because they were not known to be related. Colonel Gustaf Eneskiöld died of hunger and cold in captivity in Moscow in 1718
2 CONC . The same happened to his son Carl Magnus in Totma a year later.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the situation calmed down a bit in 1722, to everyone's surprise, captain Berndt Johan Eneskiöld, lieutenant Gustaf Eneskiöld and horse master Engelbregt von Jordan returned alive from Russia. However, the journey from Siberia
2 CONC  to the Pori region was long after 13 years of forced labor, so Berndt Johan died quite soon after returning home. Of Colonel Gustaf Enesköld's sons, only Gustaf escaped captivity alive and continued his military career as a capta
2 CONC in and regimental quartermaster until 1745, when he resigned from the army. He died on 24 October 1764.
2 CONT
2 CONT Berndt Johan, the eldest son of innkeeper Gustaf Eneskiöld, was born on October 21, 1722, but already in 1735 he fought as a volunteer soldier against the Russians, and the following year he was promoted to corporal at only 14 yea
2 CONC rs old. This shows how few armed men in Finland had become during the Big Hatred. In the years 1741 - 42, this young man took part in the rustlings of petty hatred and rose quite quickly in the officer's career. He left the army a
2 CONC s a major in 1770.
2 CONT His son Major Carl Constantin Eneskiöld (b.1767, d.1803) was aggravatingly involved in Anjala's alliance in 1788 against King Kustaa III. In Anjala's manor, the officers signed a covenant deploring the illegal start of the war. Ho
2 CONC wever, the alliance soon fell apart, and Carl Constantin, along with other officers, was captured and transported to Fredikshov, where 87 officers were sentenced to death. CarlConstantin's death sentence was never carried out, but
2 CONC  he was pardoned because of his young age. He was only 21 years old when he wrote his name in the covenant book. " (Timo Kause)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.pohjanprikaatinkilta.fi/PohPr/taistelut/Napue_1714.htm Otto Reinhold Enesköld fell as a lieutenant in the battle of Napue in 1714. If it is the same Otto Reinhold, he would have been 20 years old when he fell.
2 CONT
